  "evidence": {
    "status": "OK",
    "chars": 21561,
    "grade": "PASS",
    "ledger": [
      {
        "id": "E1",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Introducing harness adapters: adapt your favorite model to your harness to improve performance and cost",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E2",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Our earlier work ( multi-harness RL , harness adapters ) showed that RL on your own task distribution, inside your own harness, gives a more accurate and more efficient model.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E3",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "We trained one LoRA adapter for Qwen3.8-27B per agent harness (pi, Hermes Agent, OpenCode), with RL inside the harness itself.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E4",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Each harness adapter beats the base model under its own harness on both benchmarks, for both performance and efficiency.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E5",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "All four use the same agent harness (OpenCode with up to 48 model calls), the same settings and one 8×H200 Hugging Face Job, and the reward is the verifier's pass or fail.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E6",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "To obtain our final harness adapters, we trained LoRA adapters on top of Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B using TRL's AsyncGRPOTrainer .",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E7",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The final reward function consists of the verifier's correctness, combined with a small tool-efficiency bonus (0.1, derived from early experiments from the previous iteration of harness adapters which showed that 0.1 worked best).",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E8",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The agents become very short (13 tool calls per task, against 49 for the base model), and while Hermes peaks at 62.8%, pi falls back to the base model's level.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E9",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Under each harness, on the tasks every run graded (50–51 on SWE-bench Pro, 82–85 on Terminal-Bench 2.1), one attempt per task (in parentheses, the efficiency gain with respect to the base model):",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E10",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "With thinking off, the super-adapter improves on the base model on all six harness–benchmark pairs (mean pass@1 39.5% → 49.0%; 71 tasks newly solved vs 34 lost, p < 0.001).",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E11",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The adapters also solve tasks much more efficiently: on the tasks both they and the base model solve, they make 56–72% fewer tool calls per task on SWE-bench Pro and 53–70% fewer on Terminal-Bench 2.1.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E12",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "We also saw some surprising results, such as users reporting that the harness adapters work really well under Qwen3.8 derivatives, such as Swift-1.5 .",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/harness-adapters-at-scale",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:25:50.571Z"
      }
    ],
